## Rubik's Maze \'97 Initial Working Visual Prototype Plan\
\
### What exists today\
\
The `Mazen_Claude` Xcode project is a fresh **Metal 4** scaffold (created 2026-06-16) with:\
\
- **`Mazen_Claude Shared/`** \'97 `Renderer.swift` (379 lines), `Shaders.metal` (53 lines), `ShaderTypes.h`, `Assets.xcassets` with a `ColorMap` texture\
- **`Mazen_Claude macOS/`** \'97 thin `NSViewController` host, checks `supportsFamily(.metal4)`\
- **`Mazen_Claude iOS/`** \'97 thin `UIViewController` host, returns early on simulator\
\
The renderer uses the Metal 4 API surface: `MTL4CommandQueue`, `MTL4CommandBuffer`, `MTL4CommandAllocator`, `MTL4ArgumentTable`, `MTLResidencySet`, `MTL4RenderPipelineDescriptor` with `MTL4CompilerDescriptor`. It currently draws a spinning textured box using `MTKMesh` (ModelIO) with triple-buffered uniforms and a `MTLSharedEvent` fence.\
\
Both targets deploy to **macOS 26.5 / iOS 26.5**, Swift 5. Simulator is dead (`Renderer.init` returns `nil`).\
\
### Prototype goal\
\
Answer one question: **standing in the maze, rotating a slice, and watching the walls rearrange around you \'97 with fog tiles visible at the periphery \'97 does this feel right?**\
\
Success is demonstrated on a physical Mac **and** a physical iPhone/iPad, both at 60 fps. No game rules, no menus, no audio.\
\
### Locked decisions\
\
| Decision | Choice | Rationale |\
|---|---|---|\
| Graphics API | Metal 4 (keep scaffold) | Future-proof; scaffold already works. iOS requires physical device \'97 no simulator. |\
| Data model source of truth | **Cubie/facelet** | Cubies own facelets. A turn = permute cubies + quarter-turn facelet orientation. `(face,row,col)` is a derived index rebuilt when dirty. |\
| Perf target | 16.67 ms on **both** Mac + recent iOS device | Frame-time HUD from Milestone 1. |\
| Camera during slice rotation | Tile-relative | Player inherits the rotating tile's transform. The world rotates around them. |\
| Fog rendering | Layered billboards | NOT ground-parallel only \'97 must also read as corridor-filling in first-person. Camera-facing / cross-stacked approach needed. |\
| Movement gating | Both tiles' shared edges must be open | A passage exists only when tile A's edge AND tile B's edge are both open. Post-rotation mismatches are correct gameplay, not bugs. |\
\
### Coordinate conventions (lock before writing any code)\
\
| Convention | Value |\
|---|---|\
| Handedness | Right-handed (Metal standard) |\
| World axes | +X right, +Y up, +Z toward camera |\
| Face normals | +X, -X, +Y, -Y, +Z, -Z (outward from cube center) |\
| Face-local "north" | The direction toward +Y pole when projected onto the face. Top/bottom faces: north = toward -Z / +Z respectively. |\
| Tile-local origin | Center of tile, on the surface plane |\
| Winding | Counter-clockwise front-facing (scaffold already sets `.counterClockwise`) |\
\
